"""Board: cf-sz5 — Panasonic Let's Note CF-SZ5 (Skylake-U, vPro/AMT 11.8).

Identical hardware posture to //boards/hw-x64 (generic UEFI + ACPI x86-64: GOP
framebuffer, AHCI + NVMe, ACPI/PCI enumeration) — it shares that board's driver
list, bootfs staging map and feature tokens verbatim. The ONE thing this board
exists to change is the serial console, and that difference is load-bearing:

  hw-x64 uses `kernel.serial=legacy`, an alias for the COM1-compatible legacy
  UART at I/O port 0x3f8 IRQ 4 (uart::ns8250 kLegacyConfig). THE CF-SZ5 HAS NO
  COM1. Booting it with `legacy` produces a completely silent machine, which is
  indistinguishable from a hang — you cannot tell first light from a crash.

  What it does have is Intel AMT Serial-over-LAN: the ME exposes PCI function
  00:16.3 as a genuine 16550A whose bytes are tunnelled over the network to an
  `amtterm` client. It is a PIO (not MMIO) UART, measured on the actual unit at
  I/O port 0xf0a0, IRQ 19 — Linux enumerates it as ttyS4 `port=0xF0A0 type=4`
  (PORT_16550A). The SOL function is port-mapped, so the PIO driver is the
  correct one.

  The matching cmdline is therefore `ioport,<base>,<irq>`:
    * "ioport" is uart::ns8250::PioDriver's kConfigName (ns8250.h) — the config
      name for ZBI_KERNEL_DRIVER_I8250_PIO_UART.
    * the payload is zbi_dcfg_simple_pio_t, and its ParseConfig
      (//zircon/system/ulib/uart/parse.cc) reads EXACTLY two integers in the
      order (base, irq). Both accept 0x hex.

  Serial is the ONLY console that leaves this machine remotely, so this board is
  also what makes the CF-SZ5 debuggable at all: gop_display lights the internal
  panel, but nobody is sitting in front of it.

WHY THE IRQ FIELD IS 0 AND NOT 19 (measured, not stylistic):

  The SOL function's interrupt really is GSI 19, but handing that to the kernel
  as the UART IRQ PANICS IT before the console works. //zircon/kernel/platform/
  pc/uart.cc feeds the configured IRQ to apic_io_isa_to_global(), which ASSERTs
  `isa_irq < NUM_ISA_IRQS` (16) — GSI 19 is not a legacy ISA IRQ. Booting
  `ioport,0xf0a0,19` dies at:

    *** ASSERT FAILED at (zircon/kernel/arch/x86/ioapic.cc:406):
            isa_irq < NUM_ISA_IRQS

  (Reproduced under the //validation:cf-sz5-uefi-boot gate before this board was
  ever put on hardware; that assert is a landmine for any PCI-function UART, and
  pc/uart.cc now passes GSIs >= 16 through instead of asserting.)

  irq=0 is not a workaround for that bug — it is the kernel's SUPPORTED polling
  mode. platform/debug.cc's UartDriverHandoffLate treats a zero/absent IRQ as
  "no interrupt" and starts gUartPollTimer, logging "UART: POLLING mode
  enabled." That mode is fully bidirectional: TX already polls the line-status
  register's tx_register_empty bit unconditionally (uart::ns8250 TxReady/Write,
  so console output NEVER depended on the interrupt), and RX is drained by the
  poll timer instead of an RX interrupt.

  So the cost of irq=0 is input latency bounded by the poll period, not
  functionality — a good trade for a machine whose only console is remote.
  Moving to interrupt-driven RX additionally requires the IOAPIC entry for GSI
  19 to be programmed with PCI trigger/polarity (level/low) rather than the
  default edge/high, which cannot be rehearsed under QEMU (its ISA bus rejects
  irqnum >= 16 outright), so it must be proven on the real unit first.

Because AMT SOL is a real UART the kernel drives directly (not a firmware
redirection service), the port stays open for the whole boot — including after
ExitBootServices, where UEFI-console output would stop.

STATUS ON REAL HARDWARE (2026-07-25) — READ BEFORE RETRYING:

  The first attempts booted the unit but got NOTHING on Serial-over-LAN. The
  cause was found and fixed; what follows is what was actually measured, so
  the next attempt does not re-derive it (or re-chase the refuted theory at
  the end):

  * The image boots and keeps running. A plain reboot on this machine reaches
    the fallback OS in 55s (measured baseline). Booting the first-light image
    via a one-shot UEFI BootNext left the machine powered On and it NEVER came
    back to the fallback OS — twice, >4 minutes each. A boot loader that failed
    would have returned an error and fallen through in ~55-75s, which is exactly
    what the roughly 8 MB smoke image did. The image that keeps
    running is the one that starts component_manager and never terminates.

  * The SOL capture path itself WORKS, and this was proved on the same boots,
    not assumed: with Linux running, ModemManager's probe of /dev/ttyS4 shows up
    byte-for-byte in `amtterm` ("AT^SQPORT?", "AT"...). So bytes written to
    0xf0a0 by a host OS do reach the network. Cerise's do not.

  * ROOT CAUSE (found, measured on the unit, and fixed) — FIRMWARE HANDS THE OS
    PCI 00:16.3 WITH ITS I/O DECODE TURNED OFF. Instrumented physboot reported,
    on the machine:

      base=0xf0a0 irq=0 IER=ff IIR=ff LCR=ff MCR=ff LSR=ff MSR=ff SCR=ff
      pci 00:16.3 id=9d3d8086 cmd=0000 bar0=0000f0a1 pmcap=50 pmcsr=0008

    BAR0 is programmed (0xf0a1 → base 0xf0a0) and the function is in D0, but
    COMMAND = 0x0000, so nothing answers at 0xf0a0. An unclaimed x86 I/O port
    reads all-ones, so LSR bit 5 (tx-empty) looks permanently set, TxReady()
    returns true immediately, and every byte goes into a port nobody owns with
    no error reported anywhere.

    Proven in the same boot by setting the COMMAND I/O-enable bit through
    legacy 0xcf8/0xcfc config access and re-probing: the window came alive
    (IER=00 IIR=01 LCR=03 MCR=00 LSR=00 MSR=bb) and Cerise's own bytes reached
    amtterm for the first time. Corroborated from the other direction: forcing
    00:16.3 to D3hot from Ubuntu makes all seven registers read 0xff, and
    returning it to D0 makes them read real values again.

    Fixed in //zircon/kernel/phys/uart-console.cc, which enables the decode
    before Init() when a port-mapped UART reads back as an empty bus. QEMU
    cannot reproduce the failure — isa-serial has no PCI function behind it —
    which is why //validation:cf-sz5-uefi-boot passed while the real machine
    stayed silent, and also why the fix is a no-op under that gate.

    Linux only looks like it prints from [0.000000] on this machine because
    printk REPLAYS its ring buffer when ttyS4 registers at [0.446722], after
    the PCI core enabled the device at [0.425151] ("enabling device
    (0001 -> 0003)"). Nothing was on the wire before that either.

  * REFUTED, do not resurrect: "the ME holds the SOL port in loopback until a
    host OS programs the divisor". amtterm does say "SOL device is running in
    loopback mode" at session start, but that flag is SOL_CONTROLS_FROM_HOST
    (0x29) reporting LOOPBACK_ACTIVE (0x02), which mirrors the HOST's own
    MCR-LOOP bit — Linux's serial8250 autoconfig scratch test, asserted and
    cleared within milliseconds. The ME does not gate forwarding on it, and
    Cerise's silence had nothing to do with the divisor. ns8250's Init does now
    program 115200 8N1 on PIO ports, because that matches Linux and is what the
    rest of Zircon assumes, but it is not the fix and never was.

  * Ruled out by measurement, do not re-investigate: the I/O base (firmware
    assigns BAR0 = io 0xf0a0-0xf0a7 and Linux never moves it); the cmdline
    grammar and precedence (ZBI driver items are applied before ZBI cmdline
    items, so kernel.serial wins — the QEMU gate confirms the console lands on
    0xf0a0); ZBI structure (item[0] is KRNL); the ESP layout (the shim opens
    \bootx64.zbi from the volume root of its own loaded image); Secure Boot
    (disabled, platform in Setup Mode); and the boot entry's load options
    (empty, so the shim uses its DEFAULT_ZBI_FILENAME).

  * Also worth knowing: the pre-kernel shim prints on the UEFI console, and this
    machine's firmware does not redirect POST/UEFI output to SOL. So even on a
    fully working boot, the FIRST thing expected on SOL is the kernel's own
    "physload: Console configured as kernel.serial=ioport,0xf0a0,0" — never the
    shim's CERISE-SHIM-GOP-FB. Its absence is not evidence of a shim failure.
"""

load("//assembly:defs.bzl", "cerise_board")
load(
    "//boards/hw-x64:drivers.bzl",
    "HW_X64_BOOTFS",
    "HW_X64_BOOT_DRIVERS",
    "HW_X64_PROVIDED_FEATURES",
)

package(default_visibility = ["//visibility:public"])

cerise_board(
    name = "cf-sz5",
    platform = "//platforms:x64-fuchsia",
    # The hw-x64 boot-driver list, e1000 INCLUDED. Getting back to this plain
    # form is deliberate and was earned; the history matters, because the
    # failure it recovers from costs a hands-on power cycle.
    #
    # On the CF-SZ5 the I219-LM at 00:1f.6 is not just a NIC, it is the path AMT
    # Serial-over-LAN and the AMT web/KVM interfaces ride on: the ME has no
    # independent MAC/PHY on I219, it is muxed through this same LAN controller.
    # (Direct evidence: the ARP entry for the AMT address 192.168.3.69 carries
    # a8:13:74:92:54:8e, the I219-LM's own MAC, and Linux's own link-local
    # address on enp0s31f6 is fe80::aa13:74ff:fe92:548e — the same MAC.) So
    # whatever the host driver does to this device, it does to the only remote
    # console this machine has.
    #
    # WHAT WENT WRONG (measured, 2026-07-25): booted with e1000 indexed, the
    # driver bound to 00_1f_6, identified the part correctly (mac_type=25 =
    # e1000_pch_spt, did=0x156f) and ran e1000_reset_hw + e1000_init_hw +
    # e1000_setup_link on it. The boot itself was fine (`driver_manager: Bootup
    # completed.`, 6292 lines over SOL), but AMT then went away entirely — the
    # address stopped answering ICMP and the :16992 web UI stopped serving, so
    # power control was gone too and the unit needed a physical power cycle. The
    # CONTRASTING boot is what makes this attributable rather than coincidence:
    # the immediately preceding boot of the SAME image, differing only in that
    # e1000 faulted its driver host before touching the device, left AMT
    # completely healthy.
    #
    # THE ACTUAL CAUSE was not a missing manageability handshake but a plain
    # ORDERING BUG in the driver, and the same bug also explains the
    # 00:00:00:00:00:00 MAC this board reported. e1000_init_hw_ich8lan calls
    # e1000_init_rx_addrs_generic (e1000_ich8lan.c:5081), which programs
    # hw->mac.addr into RAR[0] and zeroes RAR[1..11] (e1000_mac.c:381-386). The
    # driver called e1000_read_mac_addr() AFTER init_hw, so hw->mac.addr was
    # still all-zero at that moment: init_hw faithfully wrote 00:00:00:00:00:00
    # into RAR[0] and, because e1000_rar_set_pch_lpt only sets the Address Valid
    # bit for a non-zero address (e1000_ich8lan.c:2088), left RAR[0] INVALID. On
    # a part where AMT shares the host's unicast address that deletes the only
    # receive filter AMT's packets match. RAR[1..11] are the SHRA entries the
    # vendored code itself documents as "shared between the Host and
    # manageability engine (ME)" (e1000_ich8lan.c:2069-2071).
    #
    # WHAT THE DRIVER DOES NOW (src/cerise/drivers/e1000/e1000.cc InitHardware):
    #   * sets CTRL_EXT.DRV_LOAD before the reset and clears it on Stop() —
    #     e1000e's get_hw_control()/release_hw_control();
    #   * logs FWSM, check_mng_mode and check_reset_block as CERISE-E1000-MNG;
    #   * snapshots only RAR[0] as a fallback for recovering the permanent MAC;
    #   * reads the MAC BEFORE init_hw, so init_hw REPROGRAMS RAR[0] with the
    #     real address (AV set) instead of destroying it;
    #   * leaves SHRA ownership to the shared code, whose rar_set path consults
    #     WLOCK_MAC and takes the ICH software semaphore before every host write;
    #   * never treats a later RSPCIPHY transition as permission to run the
    #     reset-time setup_link sequence against a live interface.
    #
    # Reverting to this list without those driver changes will take AMT out again.
    #
    # MEASURED WITH THE MAC-ORDERING FIX, on the unit, over six boots
    # (2026-07-25). AMT was verified healthy before each and after each — ICMP,
    # the :16992 web UI answering an authenticated digest request with HTTP 200,
    # and AMT power commands still executing. This is the historical log format
    # from that validation:
    #   CERISE-E1000-MNG fwsm=e001c21c fw_valid=1 mng=1 reset_block=12 wlock_mac=4
    #   CERISE-E1000-RAR0 pre=80008e54:927413a8 post-reset=80008e54:927413a8
    #   CERISE-E1000-RAR restored=0 of rar_count=12 post-init rar0=80008e54:927413a8
    #   e1000: MAC a8:13:74:92:54:8e
    #   CERISE-E1000-LINK status=00080483 lu=1 speed=1000 duplex=full
    # RAR[0] is byte-identical before and after the reset, which also PROVES the
    # reset never cleared it — init_hw was the destructive step, exactly as
    # described above. The zero restore count is also why removing the raw SHRA
    # replay does not change the validated path: WLOCK_MAC=4 made the shared code
    # leave the ME-reserved entries alone, while RAR[0] was reprogrammed correctly
    # because the MAC was read first.
    #
    # RESOLVED 2026-07-25 evening — NETWORKING WORKS ON THIS BOARD. Measured on
    # the unit: cerise booted from its own SSD, the driver bound the I219-LM,
    # netstack3 brought `lan-eth0` up with 172.20.10.120/24 obtained by DHCP
    # (a packet capture logged the ACK to a8:13:74:92:54:8e), NUD reached
    # Reachable for the gateway, and a test client completed a TCP connection
    # to the file server on
    # :50051. A TCP handshake and a DHCP exchange both require transmit, so the
    # descriptor stall described below is gone. AMT stayed up on the same wire
    # throughout, so the manageability coexistence holds too.
    #
    # What fixed it is not recorded here — only that the symptom is measurably
    # absent. If the stall returns, the diagnosis below is still the best map of
    # what was ruled out. Do not treat the paragraph below as current state; it
    # can waste substantial debugging time if read as if it were.
    #
    # HISTORICAL — the transmit stall as originally measured:
    # The driver binds, reports the right MAC, and brings the link up at
    # 1000/full, and netstack3 creates the interface — but DHCP DISCOVER times
    # out because THE TRANSMITTER NEVER CONSUMES DESCRIPTORS. Measured across
    # three boots: TDH stays pinned at 0 while TDT advances (e.g. 7 -> 10 -> 11)
    # and RX works throughout (RDH advances 2 -> 4 -> 5, mpc=0, rnbc=0,
    # crcerrs=0). Ruled out by direct register readback: TCTL=000400fa (EN|PSP,
    # CT/COLD set), TDBAL/TDLEN/TXDCTL correct, TIPG=00602008, link up, and both
    # halves of the i219 Tx-hang errata now applied (TARC0 2-request + IOSFPC
    # RDMTS + FEXTNVM11 DISABLE_MULR_FIX — all three were vendored in the shared
    # code but never applied by it). GPTC still counts packets, which is the ME
    # transmitting over the same MAC, so the MAC's transmit path itself is alive
    # and only the HOST descriptor queue is unserviced.
    #
    # That gap was a plain data-path bug, NOT a manageability one: it was present
    # with the ME quiescent or busy, and it cost nothing but throughput. The
    # driver was indexed here even while it persisted, because it is provably
    # safe to bind on this machine — which is the property that was actually
    # missing. (End of historical section; see RESOLVED above for current state.)
    boot_drivers = HW_X64_BOOT_DRIVERS,
    provided_features = HW_X64_PROVIDED_FEATURES,
    # AMT Serial-over-LAN 16550A at PIO 0xf0a0 (measured on the unit). IRQ field
    # is 0 = the kernel's polling mode; the real GSI is 19, see the docstring.
    kernel_cmdline = ["kernel.serial=ioport,0xf0a0,0"],
    bootfs = HW_X64_BOOTFS,
)
